<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartolomé Esteban Murillo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1617–1682</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartolomé Esteban Murillo was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> painter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, where he spent almost his entire career. He was celebrated for gentle religious paintings, especially of the Immaculate Conception, and for scenes of street children, and in 1660 he helped found an academy of painting in Seville. His soft style made him the best-known Spanish painter abroad until the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolome-esteban-murillo.jpg" alt="Bartolomé Esteban Murillo" width="320" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Autorretrato_de_Murillo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
